        artifacts:[
          {title:'Artifact A // Spending Snapshot', content:`Laptop installment .......... $420\nGas and parking ............. $188\nFood delivery ............... $94\nConcert tickets ............. $210\nGroceries and essentials .... $163\nOther small purchases ....... $87`},
          {title:'Artifact B // Paycheque Note', content:`Hours last month dropped from 22/week to 12/week for two weeks.\nMichelle expected hours to rebound, but the statement arrived before the rebound did.`}
        ]
      },
      {
        id:'slide2', short:'The Statement', title:'The Statement Does Not Just Tell Michelle What She Owes. It Tells Her What Will Happen Next.',
        prompt:'Which numbers on Michelle’s statement matter most: the balance, the minimum payment, the APR, the due date, or the available credit? Rank them and explain your order.',
        body:`
          <p>When Michelle opens her statement, she sees several numbers that look routine but actually tell very different stories. The balance shows what she owes now. The due date shows when consequences begin. The minimum payment offers short-term relief. The APR determines how expensive delay becomes. Available credit affects how much room she has left — and how lenders may judge her card use.</p>
          <p>Students often understand these numbers separately. Real life is harder because the numbers interact. A manageable balance can become expensive when paired with a high APR. A harmless-looking minimum payment can become dangerous when it hides how slowly the debt disappears. A card with space left on it can still signal risky utilization if too much of the limit is being used.</p>
          <div class="callout"><strong>Advisory lens:</strong> Michelle does not need to memorize every term again. She needs to decide which numbers deserve her attention first.</div>
        `,
        artifacts:[
          {title:'Artifact C // Statement Summary', content:`Current balance ............. $1,162.43\nCredit limit ................ $1,500.00\nAvailable credit ............ $337.57\nAPR ......................... 19.99%\nMinimum payment due ......... $35.00\nPayment due date ............ June 8`},
          {title:'Artifact D // Margin Note', content:`Michelle texted a friend: “I can definitely handle $35 this month. So I’m okay... right?”`}
        ]
      },
      {
        id:'slide3', short:'The Trap', title:'Minimum Payments Buy Time — But They Also Sell Michelle a Story About Safety.',
        prompt:'Why can a minimum payment feel psychologically reassuring even when it is financially weak? Explain how this creates a trap for Michelle.',
        body:`
          <p>Minimum payments exist for a reason: they keep an account current and can protect a borrower from missing the due date entirely. In that narrow sense, they are useful. The problem is that they can also create the illusion that debt is under control when the balance is shrinking very slowly and interest keeps accumulating.</p>
          <p>Michelle looks at the minimum and feels relief. That is understandable. Thirty-five dollars feels possible in a month where money is tight. But if the minimum becomes the plan rather than the backup, then the card stops being a tool and starts becoming an expensive extension of the problem.</p>
          <p>Financial literacy is not just about math. It is also about recognizing which numbers calm people down for the wrong reasons.</p>
        `,
        artifacts:[
          {title:'Artifact E // Scenario Check', content:`If Michelle pays only the minimum this month, the account stays current.\nIf she repeats that habit, interest charges continue and payoff stretches far longer than she expects.`},
          {title:'Artifact F // Friend Advice', content:`“Just make the minimum until summer. That’s what it’s there for.”`}
        ]
      },
      {
        id:'slide4', short:'Second Card?', title:'A New Offer Promises Rewards, Flexibility, and “Breathing Room.” That Does Not Mean It Solves Michelle’s Problem.',
        prompt:'Michelle has been pre-approved for a second card with rewards and a balance-transfer teaser. Should she consider it now, later, or not at all? Make the strongest case you can on both sides before giving your advice.',
        body:`
          <p>Michelle receives another offer in the mail. It promises a higher limit, travel points, and a low introductory rate for balance transfers. At first glance, this sounds like a solution: more space, more time, maybe even better credit-building opportunities if she manages the accounts carefully.</p>
          <p>But a second card can solve one problem while deepening another. For a disciplined borrower with a clear payoff plan, it might help reorganize debt. For a borrower already using credit to absorb financial stress, it can increase temptation, complexity, and total exposure. The card itself is not automatically wise or foolish. Context decides.</p>
          <div class="callout"><strong>Grey area:</strong> A second card is not always a mistake. The question is whether Michelle is opening it from a strategy or from pressure.</div>
        `,
        artifacts:[
          {title:'Artifact G // Promotional Mailer', content:`PRE-APPROVED\n2% cash back on groceries and gas\n0.99% intro rate on balance transfers for 6 months\n$89 annual fee after first year\nHigher spending limit subject to approval`},
          {title:'Artifact H // Michelle’s Thought', content:`“If I move the balance and free up this card, maybe I can breathe for a while.”`}
        ]
      },
      {
        id:'slide5', short:'Future Damage', title:'One Missed Payment Can Cost More Than a Fee. It Can Change How the Future Sees Michelle.',
        prompt:'Explain how late payments, high utilization, and repeated borrowing can affect Michelle’s future options even if the current problem seems manageable.',
        body:`
          <p>Michelle is also thinking about life beyond this month. She may need a car loan in the future. She may apply for an apartment lease. She may need lower-cost borrowing later for something important. Credit behaviour today can shape those future options.</p>
          <p>This is where the distinction between a credit report and a credit score becomes more than vocabulary. The report records behaviour. The score summarizes risk. Late payments, near-maxed cards, and repeated reliance on borrowed room can push lenders and landlords to see Michelle as less reliable, even if she feels only slightly behind right now.</p>
          <p>Michelle does not need perfect credit. She does need to avoid turning a temporary squeeze into a pattern that follows her into future decisions.</p>
        `,
        artifacts:[
          {title:'Artifact I // Risk Snapshot', content:`Current utilization on main card: about 77%\nOne missed payment could trigger interest, a penalty fee, and possible score damage.\nHigh utilization can make lenders think Michelle is leaning heavily on borrowed money.`},
          {title:'Artifact J // Future Goals', content:`Michelle wants: 1) stable summer work, 2) the option of a better car next year, 3) enough credit trust to rent without extra stress.`}
        ]
      },
      {
        id:'slide6', short:'Advisor Report', title:'Michelle Does Not Need a Lecture. She Needs a Plan.',
        prompt:'Write Michelle a clear advisory report. Identify her three biggest risks, one thing she did that is still recoverable, and a 30-day plus 90-day action plan.',
        body:`
          <p>At this point, your job is no longer to define credit terms. It is to advise Michelle in a practical sequence. The strongest answers will not only tell her what not to do. They will show her what to do first, what to postpone, and how to keep one rough month from becoming an expensive cycle.</p>
          <p>You may conclude that Michelle should avoid a second card for now. You may conclude that a carefully used transfer offer could help, but only under strict conditions. Either answer can work if it is evidence-based and realistic. What matters is whether your plan actually reduces risk instead of just rearranging it.</p>
          <div class="callout"><strong>Final task:</strong> Build a plan Michelle could follow tomorrow morning, not just a speech about responsibility.</div>
        `,
        artifacts:[
          {title:'Artifact K // Required Final Report Elements', content:`1. Biggest risk factors\n2. Short-term payment strategy\n3. Recommendation about second card\n4. 30-day plan\n5. 90-day plan\n6. One sentence Michelle could remember when tempted to use credit casually again`}
        ]
      }
    ];

    const state = { current:0, student:null, responses:{} };

    const $ = (id)=>document.getElementById(id);

    function setThemeToggle(){
      const root=document.documentElement;
      const btn=document.querySelector('[data-theme-toggle]');
      btn.addEventListener('click',()=>{
        const next=root.getAttribute('data-theme')==='dark'?'light':'dark';
        root.setAttribute('data-theme',next);
      });
    }

    function buildNav(){
      const nav=$('nav');
      nav.innerHTML='';
      slides.forEach((slide,index)=>{
        const btn=document.createElement('button');
        btn.id=`nav-${slide.id}`;
        btn.innerHTML=`<span>${String(index+1).padStart(2,'0')} — ${slide.short}<small>${slide.title}</small></span><span class="done" id="done-${slide.id}"></span>`;
        btn.addEventListener('click',()=>loadSlide(index));
        nav.appendChild(btn);
      });
    }

    function renderArtifacts(items){
      $('artifactPanel').innerHTML = items.map(item=>`
        <section class="artifact">
          <h4>${item.title}</h4>
          <div class="mono">${item.content}</div>
        </section>
      `).join('');
    }

    function markCompleted(){
      slides.forEach(slide=>{
        const done = $('done-'+slide.id);
        if(done) done.textContent = state.responses[slide.id] ? 'Saved' : '';
      });
    }

    function loadSlide(index){
      state.current=index;
      const slide=slides[index];
      $('slideId').textContent=`FILE ${String(index+1).padStart(2,'0')}`;
      $('slideTitle').textContent=slide.title;
      $('slideBody').innerHTML=slide.body;
      $('promptText').textContent=slide.prompt;
      $('studentResponse').value=state.responses[slide.id] || '';
      $('topTitle').textContent=slide.short + ' // ' + slide.title;
      renderArtifacts(slide.artifacts);
      document.querySelectorAll('.nav button').forEach(btn=>btn.classList.remove('active'));
      $('nav-'+slide.id).classList.add('active');
      $('nextBtn').textContent=index === slides.length - 1 ? 'Stay on final file' : 'Next file';
      markCompleted();
    }

    async function api(action,payload={}){
      if(!API_URL || API_URL.includes('PASTE_YOUR_DEPLOYED_WEB_APP_URL_HERE')){
        return {ok:true, localOnly:true, responses:state.responses};
      }
      const res = await fetch(API_URL, {
        method:'POST',
        headers:{'Content-Type':'application/json'},
        body:JSON.stringify({action, simulation:SIMULATION_NAME, ...payload})
      });
      return res.json();
    }

    async function startSession(){
      const block=$('studentBlock').value.trim();
      const name=$('studentName').value.trim();
      const passcode=$('studentCode').value.trim().toUpperCase();
      const error=$('loginError');
      error.textContent='';
      if(!name){ error.textContent='Please enter a student name.'; return; }
      if(!/^[A-Za-z]{4}$/.test(passcode)){ error.textContent='Passcode must be exactly 4 letters.'; return; }
      $('syncTag').textContent='Sync connecting';
      try{
        const result = await api('initSession',{block,name,passcode});
        if(!result.ok){ throw new Error(result.message || 'Unable to start session.'); }
        state.student={block,name,passcode};
        state.responses=result.responses || {};
        $('studentTag').textContent = `${name}${block ? ' // ' + block : ''}`;
        $('syncTag').textContent = result.localOnly ? 'Local only' : 'Sync ready';
        $('loginScreen').classList.add('hidden');
        $('briefingOverlay').classList.remove('hidden');
        markCompleted();
      }catch(err){
        $('loginError').textContent=err.message;
        $('syncTag').textContent='Sync error';
      }
    }

    async function saveCurrent(final=false){
      if(!state.student){ return; }
      const slide=slides[state.current];
      state.responses[slide.id]=$('studentResponse').value.trim();
      markCompleted();
      $('syncTag').textContent=final ? 'Submitting...' : 'Saving...';
      try{
        const result = await api(final ? 'submitFinal' : 'saveProgress', {
          block:state.student.block,
          name:state.student.name,
          passcode:state.student.passcode,
          slideId:slide.id,
          responses:state.responses
        });
        if(!result.ok){ throw new Error(result.message || 'Save failed'); }
        $('syncTag').textContent = final ? 'Final saved' : 'Saved';
      }catch(err){
        $('syncTag').textContent='Save error';
        alert(err.message);
      }
    }

    function wireEvents(){
      $('startBtn').addEventListener('click',startSession);
      $('beginInvestigation').addEventListener('click',()=>{
        $('briefingOverlay').classList.add('hidden');
        loadSlide(0);
      });
      $('saveBtn').addEventListener('click',()=>saveCurrent(false));
      $('nextBtn').addEventListener('click',()=>{
        saveCurrent(false);
        if(state.current < slides.length-1) loadSlide(state.current+1);
      });
      $('finalSubmitBtn').addEventListener('click',()=>saveCurrent(true));
      $('studentCode').addEventListener('input',e=>{ e.target.value=e.target.value.toUpperCase().replace(/[^A-Z]/g,'').slice(0,4); });
    }

    buildNav();
    setThemeToggle();
    wireEvents();
  </script>
</body>
</html>
